<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="section-ht-chi-squared" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Multiple Proportions and <m>\Xi^2</m> Distributions</title>
  <p> 
    There are yet other types of statistics to test that I have not
    already introduces. For categorical variables, where the
    statistics are proportions, I've considered the difference in
    proportions and a single proportion. You can think of these as
    trials: a difference in proportion is for a controled trial, where
    a control group is compared against a treatment group; and a
    single proportion is trial without a control group where we
    compare against a threshold.
  </p>
  <p>
    But what if there are more than just two groups
    in the trial? What if, for example, I wanted to study the rate of
    high school graduation across several major Canadian cities. There
    still is success and failre, graduatation and not graduation. But
    instead of control and treatment, I now have many categories:
    Montreal, Toronto, Winnipeg, Calgary, Edmonton, Vancouver, perhaps
    more. This is the situation of multiple proportions. Each group
    has a proportion, but there are more than two groups and I want to
    compare them all. 
  </p>
  <p>
    Even more, I could consider various level of education: high
    school graduate, dipolma, degreee, graduate degrees, and then
    compare these across the same list of cities. I would now get even
    more proportions: each city will have a proportion for each level
    of education, and I can compare all these against each other. 
  </p>
  <p>
    In this context, I want to do hypothesis testing. But what even is
    a hypothesis in this context? It's a bit tricky. Consider, first,
    some example data about lizards that we used earlier in the term
    in a class activity. 
  </p>
  <p>
    This is data about where lizards were found resting. There were two
    categorical variables, but each had three categories. Here is the
    contingency table, to remind you. One variable is the habitat, and
    one variable is the typical amount of shade in which the lizards
    rested. 
  </p>
  <table xml:id="table-lizard-data">
    <title>Lizard Location Data</title>
    <tabular>
      <row bottom="medium">>
        <cell> </cell> 
        <cell> Sun </cell> 
        <cell> Partial Shade </cell> 
        <cell> Shade </ceill>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell> Desert </cell> 
        <cell> 16 </cell> 
        <cell> 32 </cell> 
        <cell> 71 </ceill>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell> Valley </cell> 
        <cell> 42 </cell> 
        <cell> 40 </cell> 
        <cell> 24 </ceill>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell> Mountain </cell> 
        <cell> 56 </cell> 
        <cell> 36 </cell> 
        <cell> 15 </ceill>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>
  <p>
    There are nine total proportions here. For example, <m>51.9\%</m>
    of the mountain habitat lizards were found resting in full sun,
    and <m>37.5\%</m> of valley habitat lizards were found resting in
    partial shade. I could compare two of these in a difference or
    proportions, but I want to know about the difference across all
    nine. I'm asking a general question here: how much does habitat
    affect the choice of sun or shade in where the lizards rest? This
    question includes, implicitly, all nine proportions. The answer
    I'm looking for is, roughly, binary: either the habitat has a
    strong effect or a negligible effect. This is a setup for
    hypothesis test. 
  </p>
  <p>
    The lizard data is a good demonstration of the research question,
    but I want something a bit simpler to use as an example to develop
    the theory. Here is a  study about consumer inquiry and purchasing
    confidence. If you are purchasing something, particular outside of
    a formal store (i.e. someplace like Facebook Marketplace), how can
    you figure out if the item being sold is in good condition? In
    particular, does the particular way in which you ask your
    questions made a difference?
  </p>
  <p>
    A study was setup were 219 participants were asked to sell a used
    iPod which they knew had frozen twice in the past. The buyers got
    to keep some of the sale price, so they were motivated to recover
    decent value selling the iPod. (Forgive, again, the dated study. I
    know iPod died as a thing years ago, but this was a study from a
    good while back.  Work with me here!)
  </p>
  <p>
    Unbeknownst to the sellers, the buyers were also part of the
    study. They were instruced to ask questions to determine the
    condition of the iPod. They were told to randomly choose between
    these three questions
    <ul>
      <li>
        What can you tell me about it?
      </li>
      <li>
        It doesn't have any problems, does it?
      </li>
      <li>
        What problems does it have?
      </li>
    </ul>
  </p>
  <p>
    The idea here, psychologically, is that the first question seem
    neutral, the second has an implicitly positive assumption that
    there aren't problems, and the third has an implicitly negative
    assumption, that there are problems. The research question is
    this: do these assumptions, built into the question, have an
    effect on the responses? The data is found in the following
    contingency table. 
  </p>
  <table xml:id="table-marketplace-data">
    <title>Sellers Responses</title>
    <tabular>
      <row bottom="medium">>
        <cell> Question </cell>
        <cell> Disclose </cell>
        <cell> Hide </cell>
        <cell> Total </cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell> Neutral </cell>
        <cell> 2 </cell>
        <cell> 71 </cell>
        <cell> 73 </cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell> Positive </cell>
        <cell> 23 </cell>
        <cell> 50 </cell>
        <cell> 73 </cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell> Negative </cell>
        <cell> 36 </cell>
        <cell> 37 </cell>
        <cell> 73 </cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell> Total </cell>
        <cell> 61 </cell>
        <cell> 36 </cell>
        <cell> 219 </cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>
  <p>
    What does this data show us? Each of the questions was asked
    exactly <m>73</m> times.  Only twice did the neutral question lead
    to disclose of the potential freezing problems. The positive
    assumption led to <m>23</m> disclosures and the negative
    assumption led to <m>36</m> disclosures.
  </p>
  <p>
    The research question here is this: does the form of the question
    make a difference? But there are three options, not just two. How
    do we do the stats to answer this question? We now want to know
    not just was there a difference, but what difference is due to
    which type of questions.
  </p>
  <p>
    I want to do hypothesis testing, so I'm going to need some
    baseline information in order to setup my null hypothesis. Let me
    first look at how many disclosures there were in total. I add up
    the disclosures and divide by the number of cases, and I get a
    general disclosure rate of <m>27.8\%</m>. Mutliplying by
    <m>73</m>, this means that if all is equal, there should be about
    <m>22</m> disclosures in each of the three groups of <m>73</m>
    cases. (Technically, <m>22.33</m> disclouse, which I will use
    later.) Similarly, I could calculate that there should be about
    <m>53</m> non-disclosure in each group of <m>73</m>.
  </p>
  <p>
    The null hypothesis is that the questions make no difference at
    all, that in the whole population, roughly <m>22</m> out of
    <m>73</m> people would disclose and the difference between these
    groups in this sample is just random. Again, I want to use
    probability theory to ask how likely the sample is under this null
    hypothesis. 
  </p>
  <p>
    To test the likeliness of our sample, I need a sample statistic.
    That's a bit tricky here, with multiple proportions. I want to
    measure how much my sample differs from the null hypothesis. I
    have a baseline of <m>22.33</m> disclosures and <m>52.67</m>
    non-disclosures. I want to compare this baseline to my sample. I
    have six situations: three groups and two options for each group
    (disclose or not disclose). Each of these six situation is a
    proportion. For all of the six, I use the following formula to
    measure the difference between the sample outcome and the baseline
    I just established. (In this formula, <m>x</m> is the proportion
    from the sample and <m>b</m> is the baseline propotion from the
    null hypothesis.)
    <md>
      \frac{(x - b)^2}{b}  
    </md>
    Why this formula? It is the difference, which is good since I want
    to measure the difference. Squaring the difference means that all
    differences are positive, which is again good since I'm not
    concern here about the direction of the difference. Then dividing
    by the null hypothesis proportion scales this difference relative
    to that base. 
  </p>
  <p>
    I can apply this formula to all six of the proportions in the
    data. Here are those calculations.
    <md>
      <mrow>
        \frac{(2 - 20.33)^2}{20.33} = 16.53
      </mrow>
      <mrow>
        \frac{(23 - 20.33)^2}{20.33} = 0.35
      </mrow>
      <mrow>
        \frac{(36 - 20.33)^2}{20.33} = 12.08 
      </mrow>
      <mrow>
        \frac{(71 - 52.67)^2}{52.67} = 6.38
      </mrow>
      <mrow>
        \frac{(50 - 52.67)^2}{52.67} = 0.14
      </mrow>
      <mrow>
        \frac{(37 - 52.67)^2}{52.67} = 4.66
      </mrow>
    </md>
    These are a measure of how much each proportion differs from the
    null hypothesis baseline proportion. Then I add all these
    together. 
    <md>
      \Chi^2 = 16.53 + 0.35 + 12.08 + 6.38 + 0.14 + 4.66 = 40.13
    </md>
    This is a <m>\Chi^2</m> statistic, using the greek letter
    <sq>Chi</sq>. (This is not a roman <sq>X</sq>, even though it
    looks almost identical.) It is the standard way to measure how
    much a set of proportions (of any size) differs from a baseline
    proportion. THis setup comes from probability theory and that
    theory, for various mathematical reasons, has concluded that this
    is the preferred measurement. We can all this the <term>total
    variance</term> in the sample. 
  </p>
  <p>
    This is the sample statistic. What do we do with it? How do we use
    the <m>\Chi^2</m> to answer the hypothesis testing question?
    As before, we can do a randomization process. In this case, the
    null hypothesis is that all categories are equally likely. To
    emulate that, we can just shuffle the data: take the 61 discloe
    and the 18 hide and then just re-destribute them among the groups.
    Then, each of these instances would have its own <m>\Chi^2</m> value
    and I could calculate those, one by one. This would give me a
    distribution, and then I could compare that dsitribution with the
    sample statistic, to see how likely it is. This is the same
    randomization process I used previously, just with more categories
    to shuffle between.
  </p>
  <figure xml:id="figure-chi-squared-histogram">
    <caption>Histogram of <m>\Chi^2</m> randomization</caption>
    <image width="80%">
      <latex-image>
        \begin{tikzpicture}
          \begin{axis}[
            xlabel={$X^2$ test statistics of random samples},
            ylabel={Count}
          ]
            \addplot+ [
              hist={bins=5},
              fill=orange,
              draw=black,
              mark=none
            ]
            table [y index=0] {../data/chisquared.csv};
          \end{axis}
        \end{tikzpicture}
      </latex-image>
    </image>
  </figure>
  <p>
    <xref ref="figure-chi-squared-histogram"/> is a histogram of a
    bunch of randomizations of this data and their <m>\Chi^2</m> scores.
    This is assuming the null hypothesis: that there is no difference
    between groups. <m>\Chi^2 = 0</m> would recover this perfect
    balance, but that basically never happens. Instead, almost all of
    the sample have some variables, but most have a low <m>\Chi^2</m>.
    The random samples are groups near 0 and they decay away.  Our
    sample statistics had a <m>\Chi^2</m> of 40, which is way, way out
    compared to these randomizations. Like, this means that something
    significant is happening: these randomizations don't seem to have
    any chance at all of producing a <m>\Chi^2</m> of 40. 
  </p>
  <p>
    That would be doing the randomizations by hand. I don't want to do
    that. I want to make use of the fact that many clever
    mathematicians have build probability models so that I can just
    calculate directly. Happily, this is true, but the models are now
    different. This is no longer a normal distribution at all. The
    previous histogram didn't look normal at all: it started with lots
    of data and then decayed away only in the positive direction.
    There were no negatives at all (indeed, <m>\Chi^2</m> is always a
    positive number).
  </p>
  <p>
    The theory here, then, it not based on normal distributions. It is
    based on another set of mathematical functions, unsurprisingly
    called <m>\Chi^2</m> distributions. The normal distributions
    difference by mean and standard deviations. For <m>\Chi^2</m> there
    is one parameter. This parameter is misleadingly called
    <term>degrees of freedom</term>. <xref
    ref="figure-eight-chi-squared"/> shows the graphs of the
    <m>\Chi^2</m>-distribution where the degrees of freedom parameter
    ranges from <m>1</m> to <m>8</m>. All of these distribution start
    at a high value at zero and then decay away in the positive
    direction. They don't exist at all below zero. 
  </p>
  <figure xml:id="figure-">
    <caption>Caption</caption>
    <image width="80%">
      <latex-image>
        \begin{tikzpicture}[domain=.001:15,samples=200,thick]
          \clip (-0.2,-0.2) rectangle (20,7);
          \foreach[count=\k,evaluate={\z=\k>2?"(0,0)--":"";\c=10*\k}] 
            \g in {sqrt(pi),1,sqrt(pi)/2,1,3/4*sqrt(pi),2,15/8*sqrt(pi),6}
              \draw[color=blue!\c!red,yscale=15] \z 
                plot (\x,{exp(ln(\x/2)*\k/2-ln(\x)-\x/2-ln(\g))});
        \end{tikzpicture}
      </latex-image>
    </image>
  </figure>
  <p>
    Like with normal distributions, understanding these mathematical
    functions allows us to do the hypothesis test directly, without
    having to do the randomizations ourselves. The central limit
    theorem said that the sampling distribution for a difference of
    proprotions (or a single proportion) is normal under
    certain reasonable conditions. The same is true here: there are
    conditions for the data to fit these distributions. I'm not going
    to get into the details of those conditions<mdash/>a well designed
    study with good data should satisfy, but you should be aware that
    these conditions do exist. 
  </p>
  <p>
    To use the theory, I need to know how to determine the degrees of
    freedom parameter. We are dealing with a categorical variables
    with multiple categories. This is actually pretty straightforant:
    the number of categories is this degree of freedom. Specifically,
    take the two cateogrical variables we have here. Take the number
    of categories in the first varible, subtract one, then multiply by
    the number of categories in the second variable, again subtrating
    one.
  </p>
  <p>
    In our cases, there were three categories of questions, so the
    first calculation here is <m>3-1</m>, and two categories of
    outcome, so <m>2-1</m> in the second calculation. The degree of
    freedom we need for this particular <m>\Chi^2</m> process is
    <m>2</m>. 
  </p>
</section>
